PRODUCT_RELEASE_PLAN_AR.md §6.4: "أنشئ ai/eval/ قبل واجهة AI: 14 سيناريو lab
+ حوادث منزوعة الهوية + حالات سلبية/ملتبسة + صياغات عربية وإنجليزية +
prompt-injection داخل DNS/hostname. افصل train/dev/test."

Built ai/eval/ - the corpus, not the AI itself (nothing here downloads or
runs a model, matching the plan's own required ordering). Generated by
ai/eval/gen/ (a small Go program, `go run ./ai/eval/gen/`) directly from
corpus/v1/*/events.json + incidents.json - every "expected" grading
criterion in a case (root cause, cited event IDs, confidence ceiling,
relations) comes from what NetRewind's own deterministic engine actually
concluded on a real lab run, never invented for this corpus.

Result: 33 cases from 18 real scenarios - 18 positive, 3 negative (a real
concluded-nothing scenario, not a fabricated absence), 3 ambiguous
(blind_gap x2, collector_down - a real incident exists but the correct
answer is still refusal), 1 adversarial (malicious_dns_name - the real
prompt-injection fixture from Phase 0), 9 de-identified variants (lab
addresses replaced with placeholder tokens). Bilingual (question_ar/
question_en) throughout. Scenario-level train/dev/test split (never
splitting one scenario's cases across sets, which would leak its own answer
between tuning and scoring).

Two real generator defects found and fixed by reading the actual output
files rather than trusting the generator's own success message:
1. Every case's ID/filename was hardcoded to end in "-positive-en"
   regardless of its actual Kind field (an ambiguous/refusal case like
   blind_gap was literally named "...-positive-en" on disk) - fixed to
   reflect the real kind.
2. collector_down's real run produced 7 near-identical "collector-not-
   watching" incidents (one per collector); the first version of the
   generator turned each into its own case, producing 8 near-duplicates
   that would have silently overweighted that one rule when scores are
   aggregated. Fixed with deduplication by rule_id within a scenario
   (43 cases -> 33 after the fix).

Honest limitations, stated in ai/eval/README.md rather than glossed over:
only one adversarial scenario exists in the corpus (and it landed in the
train split after deterministic assignment - a real held-out red-team set
is still open work); questions were written directly, not reviewed by a
native technical reviewer in either language; all scenarios come from one
synthetic lab topology, so this corpus alone cannot support a claim about
real-world messiness; no grading harness or model candidate has been built
yet - this is the yardstick, not the thing being measured.

Verification: `gofmt`/`go vet ./...` clean at repo root (ai/eval/gen is part
of the root module); full repo test suite unaffected (`go test ./...` clean,
no new test failures). No model was downloaded, no disk-space-heavy step was
taken as part of this work.
